 that changes what gets pushed."], ["YouTube wants people to stay. So videos that work on a TV, the kind you can sit back and watch for 20 or 30 minutes, get pushed harder.", "YouTube wants people to stay, so videos that work on a TV, the kind you can sit back and watch for 20 or 30 minutes, get pushed harder."], ["Now, you might be thinking: \"Bryan, I can't film a 40-minute video every week.\" You don't need to. Channels that went from 10-minute videos to 14 or 18 minutes have done well. Start by adding 3 to 5 minutes of real substance, like one more example, one more client story or one more step.", "Now, you might be thinking: \"Bryan, I can't film a 40-minute video every week.\" You don't need to. Channels that went from 10-minute videos to 14 or 18 minutes have done well. Start by adding 3 to 5 minutes of real substance, like another example, a client story or an extra step."], ["Raw is beating polished. People trust the internet less than ever, and a lot of them are sick of content that looks fake or made by AI. A real person talking straight to the camera feels more honest.", "Raw videos are beating polished ones. People trust the internet less than ever, and a lot of them are sick of content that looks fake or made by AI, so a real person talking straight to the camera feels more honest."], ["Scott Taylor's best recent video is just him drawing on an iPad. No editing at all. It got ", "Scott Taylor's best recent video is just him drawing on an iPad with no editing at all. It got "], ["YouTube is also pushing down mass-produced, repetitive AI videos, the kind where someone pumps out a hundred near-identical uploads. Using AI to help you write or edit is fine. Uploading videos that have no real person, story or opinion behind them is not.", "YouTube is also pushing down mass-produced, repetitive AI videos, the kind where someone pumps out a hundred near-identical uploads. Using AI to help you write or edit is fine, as long as there's a real person, story or opinion behind the video."], ["Views Don't Pay You. Clicks Do.", "Link Clicks Predict Sales Better Than Views"], ["This is the most important section in this email, so I'm going to be really specific.", "I'm going to be really specific in this section, because it's the one that affects your sales."], ["As I like to say, one view from a business owner can be worth 10,000 regular views. A random view pays you nothing. A view from someone who could buy from you is the whole game.", "As I like to say, one view from a business owner can be worth 10,000 regular views. Random views pay you nothing, so what you want is views from people who could buy from you."], ["Some Videos Get Views. Other Videos Get Calls.", "Videos That Get Views vs Videos That Get Calls"], ["You need both kinds. Videos that bring new people in, and videos that turn them into calls.", "You need both kinds: videos that bring new people in, and videos that turn them into calls."], ["Let's define one more number. Your ", "There's one more number you need to know. Your "], ["But look at what happens when you fix that. Videos where under 0.25% of viewers clicked made about ", "Videos where under 0.25% of viewers clicked made about "], [" each. Same kind of view counts, roughly 40 times the money.", " each. With the same kind of view counts, that's roughly 40 times the money."], ["Video with 4,200 views → Link clicks", "Link clicks on a video with 4,200 views:"], ["0.25% click → ", "0.25% click rate: "], ["0.5% click → ", "0.5% click rate: "], ["1% click → ", "1% click rate: "], ["You didn't get one extra view. You just changed how you asked. Doubling your views is hard and mostly out of your control. Doubling your link-click rate is a copywriting problem, and that's fully in your control.", "Those extra clicks come from the same views, just by changing how you ask. Doubling your views is hard and mostly out of your control, but doubling your link-click rate is a copywriting problem, which you fully control."], [" of the money. A how-to title promises to teach you something for free. The viewer learns it, feels satisfied and leaves. Nothing makes them want more.", " of the money. A how-to title promises to teach you something for free. The viewer learns it, feels satisfied and leaves, with nothing pulling them to want more."], ["Teaching is still great. Just don't put the method in the title. Put the result in the title.", "Teaching is still great, but put the result in the title and save the method for the video."], [", with \"I\", \"my\" or \"me\", got 37% of clicks and 52% of the money. \"I did this\" sells. \"Here's how to\" teaches.", ", with \"I\", \"my\" or \"me\", got 37% of clicks and 52% of the money. A title that says \"I did this\" sells, and a how-to title mostly teaches."], [", like \"a month\", \"a day\" or \"in 19 days\", made 4 to 5 times more money per view than average. \"$45,000\" is just a number. \"$45,000 a month\" is something a viewer can picture themselves in.", " made 4 to 5 times more money per view than average. A viewer can picture themselves in \"$45,000 a month\", while \"$45,000\" on its own is just a number."], [". First person, a dollar amount and a rate, in both of them.", ". Both use the first person, a dollar amount and a rate."], ["Instead of this → Try this", "Some before and after examples:"], ["How To Get More Consulting Clients → ", "Instead of \"How To Get More Consulting Clients\", try "], ["How To Price Your Services → ", "Instead of \"How To Price Your Services\", try "], ["Tips For Agency Owners → ", "Instead of \"Tips For Agency Owners\", try "], ["Is Cold Outreach Dead? → ", "Instead of \"Is Cold Outreach Dead?\", try "], ["Hiring Advice For Business Owners → ", "Instead of \"Hiring Advice For Business Owners\", try "], ["It wasn't restricted. His strategy had just stopped working, and nobody could tell which videos brought in money. So we tracked every booked call back to its video, and mixed videos that bring new people in with videos that turn them into calls.", "It turned out his channel wasn't restricted. His strategy had just stopped working, and nobody could tell which videos brought in money. So we tracked every booked call back to its video, and mixed videos that bring new people in with videos that turn them into calls."], ["Bigger channels don't usually need more ideas. They need to know which of their videos actually make money, and then make more of those.", "What bigger channels usually need is to know which of their videos actually make money, and then to make more of those."], ["And remember what actually pays you. Nobody buys your service from a half-second preview. Judge every video on engaged views, link clicks and booked calls.", "Nobody buys your service from a half-second preview, so judge every video on engaged views, link clicks and booked calls."], ["YouTube Studio gives you a lot of numbers. Most of them mix up your fans and complete strangers, so they can fool you. Here are the three that matter, and all three are based on engaged views.", "YouTube Studio gives you a lot of numbers. Most of them mix up your fans and complete strangers, so they can fool you. The three that matter are below, and all three are based on engaged views."], [". It's working.", ", because it's working."], ["☐ The title makes a claim, not a question", "☐ The title makes a claim instead of asking a question"], ["☐ The video ends on the best moment, not a goodbye", "☐ The video ends on the best moment, with no goodbye"], ["☐ I judge the video on engaged views, link clicks and calls, not the public view count", "☐ I judge the video on engaged views, link clicks and calls instead of the public view count"]], "unbold": []}, "ideas": {"id": "1zzV1Vz8aGbl24ftDvYGTMoSuV8sTb4INJ5hE9U-Lak8", "pairs": [["Most business owners spend 5 seconds on the idea and 5 hours on the edit. I'd flip that. If the idea is weak, nothing you do in editing will save the video.", "Most business owners spend 5 